INT. FISH-DRYING SHED - ICELANDIC COAST - DAWN

A long timber shed. Slatted walls leak gray sea light. Thousands of cod hang from the rafters in rows, stiff as boards. The air smells of salt and iron. A woodstove ticks in the corner.

GRANDPA ÓSKAR, 80s, wears a wool cap pulled low and a face like a cliff. He sits on an overturned crate, mending a net with fingers that don't need to look.

ELINBORG, 40s, his daughter, stands rigid by the door in a city coat. Wrong boots. She holds a folder against her chest.

KARI, 16, her son, lounges against the wall in headphones, hood up. Earbuds leaking tinny bass.

SIGRÚN, 12, Kari's sister, sits cross-legged on the floor, counting fish with her lips.

BJARNI, 50s, Óskar's other son, comes in last. Shoulders wide. A thermos in one fist. Mud to the knee.

ELINBORG
You told me it was shut down.

ÓSKAR
It was shut down. Now it's open.

BJARNI
Dad wanted to dry one more season.

ELINBORG
Dad is eighty-four.

ÓSKAR
Dad can hear you.

KARI
(not looking up)
Can we go?

ELINBORG
Kari, take those out.

KARI
Why?

ELINBORG
Because your grandfather is talking.

Kari removes one earbud. Slowly. Like a concession.

ÓSKAR
Your mother wants to sell this place.

ELINBORG
I want to talk about it.

BJARNI
That's what selling is.

SIGRÚN
Seven hundred and twelve.

BJARNI
What is?

SIGRÚN
The fish.

Nobody answers her.

ELINBORG
The offer is real. A developer from Reykjavík. They want the shoreline.

ÓSKAR
They want a hotel.

ELINBORG
They want a lot of things.

BJARNI
Dad built the racks.

ÓSKAR
I built the racks. My father built the shed.

ELINBORG
And what did his father build?

ÓSKAR
Nothing. He died on a boat.

ELINBORG
Pabbi.

ÓSKAR
Don't Pabbi me.

Silence. The stove ticks. A cod sways on its string, knocking another.

ELINBORG
Dad, please.

BJARNI ^
Dad, enough.

KARI
(quietly)
Can I say something?

ÓSKAR
You can say something.

KARI
I don't care about the hotel. I don't care about any of it. But I think... I think the smell here is the best thing I've ever smelled.

ELINBORG
Kari.

KARI
I'm serious. It smells like him. Like Afi. Like everything he's ever worn.

ÓSKAR
That is a fair description.

SIGRÚN
Seven hundred and twelve.

BJARNI
Okay, we heard you.

SIGRÚN
Seven hundred and twelve is the number Mom said the developer wants.

ELINBORG
Sigrún.

SIGRÚN
Seven hundred and twelve thousand. That's what they offered. I read it.

ELINBORG
You read my folder.

SIGRÚN
It was open.

ÓSKAR
(a thin smile)
She counts well, this one.

ELINBORG
(softer)
Pabbi. I'm not trying to take it from you.

ÓSKAR
You are a little bit.

ELINBORG
A little bit.

ÓSKAR
It's all right. Everyone takes a little.

He sets the net down. Stands. Walks to the nearest rack. Reaches up and lifts a fish from its string. Holds it out to Kari.

ÓSKAR (CONT'D)
Smell it.

Kari does. Closes his eyes.

KARI
Yeah.

ÓSKAR
Now tell me what it's worth.

KARI
I don't know.

ÓSKAR
Neither do I. That's the problem.

Elinborg laughs. Wipes her eyes with the back of her glove. Bjarni pours coffee into the thermos lid and hands it to his sister without a word.

She takes it.

Sigrún goes back to counting.
